.TH AENIGMA-VPN-SERVER 8 "2026-08-17" "aenigma 5.0.0" "Aenigma Administration Scripts"
.SH NAME
aenigma-vpn-server \- provision and start an OpenVPN server instance
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B aenigma-vpn-server
.B \-d
.I DOMAIN
.B \-p
.I PORT
.B \-a
.I ADDRESS
.B \-n
.I NETMASK
.br
.B aenigma-vpn-server
.B \-h
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B aenigma-vpn-server
installs OpenVPN and Easy-RSA if necessary, provisions a shared
certificate authority the first time it is run, issues a server
certificate for the given
.IR DOMAIN ,
writes a routed OpenVPN server configuration, and enables and starts it as
a systemd
.B openvpn@
service instance.
.PP
The script maintains a single, shared Easy-RSA PKI under
.IR /etc/openvpn/easy-rsa ,
so it can be run multiple times for different values of
.I DOMAIN
to stand up several independent VPN server instances that share the same
certificate authority. The CA and Diffie-Hellman parameters are generated
only once, on the first invocation for any domain; subsequent runs for new
domains reuse the existing CA to issue a new server certificate.
.PP
.B aenigma-vpn-server
must be run as root, since it installs packages, creates the unprivileged
.I openvpn
system user if it does not already exist, writes into
.IR /etc/openvpn " and " /var/log/openvpn ,
and manages the
.B openvpn@
systemd service.
.PP
This script provisions the VPN server and its certificates only. It does
not enable IP forwarding, configure NAT/masquerading, or open firewall
ports; those must be configured separately according to the target
environment before client traffic can be routed beyond the VPN server
itself.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-d " DOMAIN"
Name identifying this VPN server instance, typically its domain name
(e.g.
.IR example.com ).
Used as the common name for its certificate, as the systemd instance name
(\fBopenvpn@DOMAIN\fR), and to derive its configuration, certificate, and
log paths. Required.
.TP
.BI \-p " PORT"
UDP port the OpenVPN server listens on, e.g.
.IR 1194 .
Required.
.TP
.BI \-a " ADDRESS"
Base network address of the VPN's virtual subnet, e.g.
.IR 10.8.0.0 .
Required.
.TP
.BI \-n " NETMASK"
Netmask of the VPN's virtual subnet, e.g.
.IR 255.255.255.0 .
Required.
.TP
.B \-h
Display usage information and exit.
.SH INTERACTIVE PROMPTS
This script is not fully unattended.
.IP \(bu 2
On the very first run (CA creation), you will be prompted to set and
confirm a passphrase for the certificate authority's private key.
.IP \(bu 2
On every run that issues a
.B new
server certificate \(em that is, every domain not already provisioned \(em
you will be prompted for that CA passphrase again, in order to sign the
new certificate.
.PP
Running this script non-interactively (e.g. from unattended automation)
requires supplying the CA passphrase to these prompts by some other means.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success; the server certificate was issued (or already existed for a
previously provisioned domain, per below) and the
.B openvpn@
service was started.
.TP
.B 1
Invoked without root privileges, called with insufficient or invalid
arguments, or a certificate already exists for the given
.IR DOMAIN .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/openvpn/easy-rsa
Shared Easy-RSA working directory and PKI, created on first run.
.TP
.I /etc/openvpn/ca.crt
.TQ
.I /etc/openvpn/dh.pem
Certificate authority certificate and Diffie-Hellman parameters, shared by
all server instances provisioned by this script.
.TP
.IB /etc/openvpn/ DOMAIN .conf
Generated OpenVPN server configuration for this instance.
.TP
.IB /etc/openvpn/ DOMAIN
Per-instance directory holding the server's certificate
(\fIDOMAIN\fR.crt), private key (\fIDOMAIN\fR.key, mode 0600, owned by
.IR openvpn ),
TLS-auth key (\fBta.key\fR, mode 0600, owned by
.IR openvpn ),
and its
.B ifconfig-pool-persist
state file.
.TP
.IB /etc/openvpn/ccd/ DOMAIN
Client-config-dir for this instance, for per-client configuration
overrides.
.TP
.IB /var/log/openvpn/ DOMAIN
Log directory for this instance, containing its OpenVPN status log.
.SH NOTES
.IP \(bu 2
Re-running
.B aenigma-vpn-server
for a
.I DOMAIN
that has already been provisioned is refused: the script detects an
existing issued certificate for that domain and exits with an error
rather than proceeding, since regenerating the certificate request would
silently overwrite the existing private key while the subsequent signing
step would then fail against the already-issued certificate, leaving the
PKI in an inconsistent state. To reissue a certificate for an existing
domain, first revoke the existing one using
.BR easyrsa (1)
directly.
.IP \(bu 2
Server certificates and keys themselves
(\fIDOMAIN\fR.key, via \fBgen-req \(dqDOMAIN\(dq nopass\fR) are generated
without a passphrase, so the
.B openvpn@
service can start unattended on boot without manual intervention. Only the
CA private key is passphrase-protected.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR openvpn (8),
.BR easyrsa (1),
.BR systemctl (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Romulus-Emanuel Ruja <romulus.rujas@aenigma.ro>